{
  "id": "sfwgrt6",
  "title": "Chapter 10: Acids, Bases and Salts",
  "order": 10,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h532c3h",
      "content": "Acids, bases and salts are important classes of chemical compounds that play a crucial role in chemistry and daily life. These substances participate in numerous chemical reactions and are widely used in industries, laboratories and households."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "pH Scale Range",
          "value": "0 - 14",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Neutral pH",
          "value": "7.0",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Blood pH Range",
          "value": "7.35 - 7.45",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Reaction",
          "value": "Acid + Base → Salt + Water",
          "icon": "book"
        }
      ],
      "id": "d32hqs9"
    },
    {
      "id": "b14hvxn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Acids"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids produce hydrogen ions (H+) in aqueous solution and turn blue litmus red.",
        "Bases produce hydroxide ions (OH-) in aqueous solution, feel soapy to touch, and turn red litmus blue.",
        "Alkalis are water-soluble bases such as Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH) and Potassium Hydroxide (KOH).",
        "The pH scale measures the hydrogen ion concentration; values less than 7 represent acidic solutions, while values greater than 7 represent basic solutions.",
        "Neutralization is the reaction between an acid and a base to form salt and water, accompanied by evolution of heat."
      ],
      "id": "lmg7t0y"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hv3nwab",
      "content": "• Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sew2zh9",
      "content": "• They have a sour taste."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3wowsfu",
      "content": "• They turn blue litmus paper red."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "md5lkw1",
      "content": "• They react with metals to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wtj9khm",
      "content": "Examples of common acids:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0zu0gu9",
      "content": "• Hydrochloric acid (HCl)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xim3qpo",
      "content": "• Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "m0pump8",
      "content": "• Nitric acid (HNO₃)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "iuirekp",
      "content": "• Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)"
    },
    {
      "id": "3l8ai2n",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Bases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8apmyq3",
      "content": "• Bases are substances that produce hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "910ehzd",
      "content": "• They taste bitter and feel slippery."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "42s62mb",
      "content": "• They turn red litmus paper blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e9pdqq2",
      "content": "Examples of bases:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "9o0ir0l",
      "content": "• Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "jydtezr",
      "content": "• Potassium hydroxide (KOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4czg1b1",
      "content": "• Calcium hydroxide Ca(OH)₂"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Neutralization reaction is the chemical reaction between an acid and a base to produce salt and water.",
      "id": "ojupiyf"
    },
    {
      "id": "onzz9fn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Alkalis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s1zwmvp",
      "content": "Alkalis are bases that dissolve in water and produce hydroxide ions. All alkalis are bases but all bases are not alkalis."
    },
    {
      "id": "gihseu0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Properties of Acids and Bases"
    },
    {
